# NextKey — from proposal to decision to act.
# ENSv2 hackathon deployment on Sepolia. Recorded 2026-09-06.
#
# Until this run the loop stopped one step short: the agent filed a proposal,
# the enclave judged it, and a person then wrote the grant by hand. That gap is
# where a system quietly stops being a system.
#
# The order below is deliberate. The refusal comes first, because a check
# nobody has seen fail is not a check.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Access is withdrawn, so that what follows restores it by process.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs revoke visa anna.nextkey.eth

  revoking anna.nextkey.eth's access to visa.nextkey.eth
  grant record  nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf
  → 0xa6b3bc1790a5c3130479e619662ee75996b8886e46a757f6adabf62220aef5be success

PS> node scripts/nextkey.mjs open visa anna

  Error: no grant at nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf for "anna"
         — access was never given, or was revoked

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. A verdict that no longer matches the chain is refused.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The agent files a fresh proposal, which replaces the record. The verdict from
# the previous run now describes a request that is no longer there.

PS> node --env-file=.env scripts/agent.mjs propose visa anna.nextkey.eth

  requestId  0x57663ef80875b452
  → 0x50710bce5288ad654894a1ec22b6f724b8ad301142da78fcaeaa05a4c960d1bd success
  requestHash 0x7b2a1ed622c22c8eebf5b591a25c5bfcf2d1896ce61d1d05efdf53f13b8ce0f3

PS> node scripts/release.mjs check --from-log evidence/cre-decision.log   # the OLD verdict

  ✓ verdict is RELEASE                     RELEASE — quorum_and_delay_satisfied
  ✗ verdict is bound to the live request   on chain 0x7b2a1ed6…3b8ce0f3
                                           verdict  0xb74ac566…4f59ce5336
  ✗ request ids agree                      0x57663ef80875b452
  ✓ request version is supported           v1

  REFUSED: verdict is bound to the live request; request ids agree

  A hash mismatch is the interesting failure. It means the request on
  chain is not the one the enclave judged — it was replaced after the
  decision.

# This is the property worth having. An approval given for one request cannot
# be spent on another, and the check is arithmetic rather than trust: hash the
# record that is there now, compare.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. The workflow judges the current request.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> cre workflow simulate my-workflow

  [USER LOG] Decision for 0x57663ef80875b452: RELEASE (quorum_and_delay_satisfied)
  [USER LOG] Bound to on-chain request 0x7b2a1ed622c22c8eebf5b591a25c5bfcf2d1896ce61d1d05efdf53f13b8ce0f3

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. Now the same check passes, and the release is carried out.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PS> node --env-file=.env scripts/release.mjs execute --from-log evidence/cre-decision.log --as alice

  verdict     RELEASE — quorum_and_delay_satisfied
  request     0x57663ef80875b452
  ✓ verdict is RELEASE
  ✓ verdict is bound to the live request   0x7b2a1ed622c22c8eebf5b591a25c5bfcf2d1896ce61d1d05efdf53f13b8ce0f3
  ✓ request ids agree                      0x57663ef80875b452
  ✓ request version is supported           v1
  would release  visa.nextkey.eth  →  anna.nextkey.eth

  executing as alice
  grant record  nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf
  → 0xac483f31175ebe6d376cd10ab0ff613612a43bed21326617e7ad1a901ae36fd0 success

PS> node scripts/nextkey.mjs open visa anna

  visa.nextkey.eth  opened as anna
  witch collapse practice feed shame open despair creek road again ice least

# Anna can open the secret again, and nobody typed `share`. An agent proposed,
# an enclave decided, and the decision was checked against the chain before it
# was acted on.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What this enforces, and what it does not
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Enforced here: a verdict must say RELEASE, and its hash must equal the hash of
# the request that is on chain at the moment of acting. PENDING and DENY are
# answers, not obstacles, and are refused the same way a mismatch is.
#
# NOT enforced: nothing stops the owner from ignoring all of this and calling
# `nextkey.mjs share` directly. The owner holds the key and the ENS role — that
# is the design, and no script can revoke it.
#
# In production the DON's signed report would be delivered on chain and a
# contract would gate the write, so the check would be enforced by the chain
# rather than by a file on someone's laptop. That step is not built. What is
# built is the decision, its binding to a public request, and a client that
# refuses to act when the two disagree — which is worth having, and is not the
# same thing as being unable to act otherwise.
